1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,664
Every monsoon, Indian cities ask citizens to send flood

2
00:00:03,664 --> 00:00:04,131
photos.

3
00:00:04,671 --> 00:00:08,087
Gurugram's flood portal even asks people to guess the water

4
00:00:08,087 --> 00:00:09,071
depth themselves.

5
00:00:09,533 --> 00:00:13,291
That means hundreds of photos and guesses, and someone has to

6
00:00:13,291 --> 00:00:15,570
decide which street needs help first.

7
00:00:16,056 --> 00:00:17,523
This is flood-triage.

8
00:00:18,005 --> 00:00:21,829
It reads each photo using Meta's open-weight model, Muse Spark

9
00:00:21,829 --> 00:00:25,468
Open, and ranks the reports worst first, so an officer sees

10
00:00:25,468 --> 00:00:27,257
the deepest water at the top.

11
00:00:27,928 --> 00:00:28,568
Here's a report.

12
00:00:28,958 --> 00:00:32,199
Instead of guessing centimetres, it judges the water against

13
00:00:32,199 --> 00:00:33,009
the car itself:

14
00:00:33,567 --> 00:00:36,676
below the tyre, up to the door, or above the windows.

15
00:00:37,114 --> 00:00:38,366
Levels zero to four.

16
00:00:38,874 --> 00:00:40,000
It shows its evidence:

17
00:00:40,430 --> 00:00:43,290
where it found the vehicle and where the waterline touches it.

18
00:00:43,774 --> 00:00:47,538
And when a photo is too dark, blurry or has no vehicle, it

19
00:00:47,538 --> 00:00:51,562
says can't tell and sends it to a person, instead of guessing.

20
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:53,193
Now the whole queue.

21
00:00:53,687 --> 00:00:56,431
Reports are sorted by severity and shown on a map.

22
00:00:56,978 --> 00:01:00,793
The agent also checks each citizen's own depth estimate

23
00:01:00,793 --> 00:01:04,747
against the photo, flags the ones that don't match, spots

24
00:01:04,747 --> 00:01:08,701
possible duplicates, and drafts a short note for the ward

25
00:01:08,701 --> 00:01:09,256
officer.

26
00:01:09,649 --> 00:01:11,178
The officer stays in charge.

27
00:01:11,555 --> 00:01:12,238
This is a triage hint, not a warning.

28
00:01:12,752 --> 00:01:16,881
We tested it on 340 held-out photos from a public flooded-car

29
00:01:16,881 --> 00:01:20,806
dataset, tuning only on the validation set and scoring the

30
00:01:20,806 --> 00:01:21,754
test set once.

31
00:01:22,125 --> 00:01:26,292
A standard image classifier scored 0.619 macro F1.

32
00:01:26,751 --> 00:01:31,213
Our hybrid, where the classifier handles easy photos and Spark

33
00:01:31,213 --> 00:01:34,163
decides the uncertain ones, scored 0.647.

34
00:01:34,606 --> 00:01:39,053
More importantly, it cut the dangerous mistakes, being off by

35
00:01:39,053 --> 00:01:41,531
two levels or more, from 29 to 17.

36
00:01:42,010 --> 00:01:45,601
Spark on its own makes even fewer of those, but it tends to

37
00:01:45,601 --> 00:01:47,913
call water one level lower than it is.

38
00:01:48,302 --> 00:01:51,876
It doesn't measure depth in centimetres, vehicle sizes vary,

39
00:01:51,876 --> 00:01:54,199
and it was mostly tested on car photos.

40
00:01:54,588 --> 00:01:58,164
We also found the dataset's official split reuses the same

41
00:01:58,164 --> 00:02:00,691
source photos, and we report that openly.

42
00:02:01,138 --> 00:02:04,420
Because Spark is open weight, a city can run it on its own

43
00:02:04,420 --> 00:02:06,796
servers and keep citizens' photos private.

44
00:02:07,390 --> 00:02:08,135
flood-triage.

45
00:02:08,532 --> 00:02:11,671
Built during the hackathon, code and results on GitHub.

46
00:02:12,209 --> 00:02:13,046
Thanks for watching.

